// Bug 37h7n3: a Map[Str, V] reached through a generic-struct field erased its
// VALUE type — typecheck does not monomorphize struct instances, so the field's
// value defaults to the erased CT_VOID. Each `b.m` field re-emit re-registered
// the map var with that erased value, clobbering the concrete type stored at
// insert, so get() emitted an int reinterpretation of the stored Str pointer.
// Interpolation renders the recovered value, exposing the int-vs-Str carrier.
//
// FLOOR PROBE (nqq29j): both cases below formerly monomorphized to the SAME
// blink_GVMap_Void — two logically distinct instances (V=Str, V=Int) sharing one
// #ifndef BLINK_TD_ typedef guard. Expected _Void count for this file:
//   blink build --emit c tests/test_37h7n3_map_field_value_type.bl -o .tmp/f
//   grep -c '_Void' .tmp/f.c
// must be 0 (was 7); the two now spell GVMap_Str and GVMap_Int. Fix: RESOLVING the slot
// (tc_tid_resolved) before asking about it lets the tid-native mono path see a bound
// metavar's real shape. Historically that read went through a resolving twin of
// tc_tid_ct, deleted with its last caller; the live gate is tc_tid_resolved +
// tc_struct_slot_encodable in tid_native_struct_mono_slots.
// Grep _Void: the retired csvresolve channel missed this class entirely. This file
// was also one of the last two string-CSV holdouts — the inferred `GVMap { m: Map() }`
// slot is a metavar bound IN PLACE, which mono_instance_arg_tids_from_tid used to read
// RAW (still TK_UNKNOWN, so unencodable) while the gate above it read the same slot
// RESOLVED. It now resolves on both sides, so the slot carries a real tid.

type GVMap[V] { m: Map[Str, V] }

test "map field value type preserved for Str value" {
    let b = GVMap { m: Map() }
    b.m.insert("k", "hello")
    assert_eq("got: {b.m.get(\"k\").unwrap()}", "got: hello")
}

test "map field Int value still works" {
    let b = GVMap { m: Map() }
    b.m.insert("n", 42)
    assert_eq("got: {b.m.get(\"n\").unwrap()}", "got: 42")
}
